### Decomposing Monoliths: Module Federation Internals

Modern large-scale web applications often outgrow single monolithic repositories. While monolithic frontends simplify initial development, cross-team coupling frequently degrades build times and slows deployment cadences.

#### Architectural Trade-offs
- **Independent Deployment**: Teams release features autonomously without coordinating global builds.
- **Shared Dependencies**: Run-time deduplication prevents users from downloading redundant copies of core libraries.
- **Contract Boundaries**: Strict TypeScript interfaces govern communication between host and remote containers.

```javascript
// Module Federation Configuration in Webpack / Vite
export default {
  name: 'dashboard_host',
  remotes: {
    analyticsApp: 'analytics@https://cdn.devpath.io/remotes/analytics/remoteEntry.js',
    billingApp: 'billing@https://cdn.devpath.io/remotes/billing/remoteEntry.js'
  },
  shared: {
    react: { singleton: true, requiredVersion: '^18.2.0' },
    'react-dom': { singleton: true, requiredVersion: '^18.2.0' }
  }
};
```

#### Fault Tolerance and Fallback Handling
When consuming remote modules over high-latency or unpredictable networks, client-side boundaries must implement robust fallback lifecycles.

```javascript
async function loadRemoteMicrofrontend(scope, module) {
  try {
    // Initialize the container
    await window[scope].init(__webpack_share_scopes__.default);
    const factory = await window[scope].get(module);
    return factory();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Failed to resolve remote module: ${scope}/${module}`, error);
    return () => import('./components/GracefulFallbackView.js');
  }
}
```

#### Key Engineering Principles
1. Ensure shared libraries specify exact semantic version constraints.
2. Isolate runtime errors within micro-frontend boundaries to avoid crashing host shells.
3. Implement localized state management and avoid global window mutations.

