### Defeating Specificity Wars with Native @layer

Cascade Layers provide explicit control over stylesheet precedence without resorting to high-specificity ID selectors or `!important` declarations.

#### Declaring Precedence Order
```css
@layer reset, base, layout, components, utilities;

@layer components {
  .card-header {
    background-color: #f8fafc;
    color: #1e293b;
  }
}

@layer utilities {
  /* Utilities win over components regardless of selector specificity */
  .text-white {
    color: #ffffff;
  }
}
```

Styles declared in unlayered CSS always take precedence over layered CSS for normal declarations, while `!important` declarations invert the layer hierarchy.

codeExercise.initialCode:
const layers = ['reset', 'base', 'components', 'utilities'];
function getWinningLayer(layerA, layerB) {
  const indexA = layers.indexOf(layerA);
  const indexB = layers.indexOf(layerB);
  return indexA > indexB ? layerA : layerB;
}

console.log('Winning layer:', getWinningLayer('components', 'utilities'));

