### Strict Nonce-Based Content Security Policy

A modern Content Security Policy does not rely on domain allowlists, which can often be bypassed via JSONP endpoints or CDN script hosting. Instead, strict nonce-based policies guarantee that only scripts bearing a cryptographically random, per-request nonce can execute.

```http
Content-Security-Policy: 
  default-src 'none';
  script-src 'nonce-r4nd0mStr1ng' 'strict-dynamic';
  style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
  font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com;
  img-src 'self' data: https:;
  connect-src 'self' https://api.devpath.io;
  base-uri 'none';
  form-action 'self';
  frame-ancestors 'none';
```

- `frame-ancestors 'none'`: Hardens the platform against clickjacking by refusing iframe embedding.
- `'strict-dynamic'`: Enables trusted scripts with the valid nonce to load child dependencies without requiring explicit domain allowlists.

codeExercise.initialCode:
function validateCSPDirective(cspString) {
  const directives = cspString.split(';').map(d => d.trim());
  const hasClickjackingDefense = directives.some(d => d.startsWith("frame-ancestors 'none'"));
  return { defendedAgainstClickjacking: hasClickjackingDefense };
}

const policy = "default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'";
console.log(JSON.stringify(validateCSPDirective(policy)));

function verifyHashLength(integrityTag) {
  const [algorithm, hash] = integrityTag.split('-');
  return { algorithm, validLength: hash.length > 20 };
